#. Tag: title
#: editing.docbook:229
#, no-c-format
msgid "ISearch"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: primary
#: editing.docbook:230
#, no-c-format
msgid "isearch"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: primary
#: editing.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid "incremental search"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: primary
#: editing.docbook:232 editing.docbook:249
#, no-c-format
msgid "search"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: secondary
#: editing.docbook:232
#, no-c-format
msgid "incremental"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The conventional search with <menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu> "
"<guimenuitem>Find</guimenuitem></menuchoice> requires you to specify the "
"full search term before starting. Most of the time, it is much faster to "
"search incrementally. If you click into the edit field labeled "
"<guilabel>ISearch</guilabel> in the toolbar, the search is performed as you "
"type. You will find that often the desired term is already found after "
"typing in 3 or 4 letters."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editing.docbook:248
#, no-c-format
msgid "Grep"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: secondary
#: editing.docbook:249
#, no-c-format
msgid "in files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:251
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Both search mechanisms described above are restricted to searching within "
"one source file. An additional tool which allows you to search through a "
"(possibly large) number of files is available through the "
"<guimenuitem>Search in Files...</guimenuitem> item in the <guimenu>Edit</"
"guimenu> menu. It is basically a frontend for the &grep;(1) program."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:260
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the dialog, you can specify which files are searched. There is a number "
"of wildcard patterns available in a combobox. In this way, you can easily "
"restrict the find mechanism to header files. Furthermore, you specify a "
"directory where the search is started. If you check the "
"<guibutton>Recursive</guibutton> box, the search iterates through all "
"directories in the hierarchy below this one."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:269
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The search term is in general a regular expression following POSIX syntax. "
"For example, you can use the term <literal>\"\\&lt;K.*\"</literal> if you "
"want to find all words which begin with the letter K. The following "
"characters are interpreted in a special way:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:281
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>.</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:282
#, no-c-format
msgid "Matches any character"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:286
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>^</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:287
#, no-c-format
msgid "Matches the beginning of a line"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:291
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>$</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:292
#, no-c-format
msgid "Matches the end of a line"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:296
#, no-c-format
msgid "\\&lt;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:297
#, no-c-format
msgid "Matches the beginning of a word"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:301
#, no-c-format
msgid "\\&gt;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:302
#, no-c-format
msgid "Matches the end of a word"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:306
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>?</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:307
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item matches less than once"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:311
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>*</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:312
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item is matched zero or more times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>+</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:317
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item is matched once or more times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:321
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>{n}</literal>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:322
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item is matched exactly n times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid "{n,}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:327
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item is matched n or more times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:331
#, no-c-format
msgid "{,n}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:332
#, no-c-format
msgid "The preceding item matches less than n times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literal
#: editing.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid "{n,m}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editing.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The preceding item matches at least <literal>n</literal> times but less than "
"<literal>m</literal> times"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:346
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Backreferences to bracketed subexpressions are also available by the "
"notation <literal>\\n</literal>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For C++ programmers, as special bonus there are some search templates "
"available for typical patterns. These allow you to search for example all "
"calls of member functions of a certain object."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editing.docbook:357
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you start the search by clicking on the <guibutton>Search</guibutton> "
"button, it will be performed by an external, asynchronous process. All found "
"items will appear in the view called <guilabel>Grep</guilabel>. You can then "
"jump to the found items by clicking on them. Note that grep scans the files "
"as they stored in the file system. If you have modified versions of them in "
"your editor, you may get some mismatches in the line number, or some found "
"items will be invalid. You can avoid this by saving all files beforehand."
msgstr ""

